<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Nutrition – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nutrition</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Healthy Food for a Balanced Diet</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain how to follow and prepare recipes. Interactive: Respond to peers in group activities.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain steps to prepare a simple healthy meal (e.g., "To make a Panamanian-style fruit salad, chop fresh pineapples, mangos, and papayas. Then, add a squeeze of orange for extra flavor.") Can express opinions about food choices (e.g., "I think fruits are the best choice because they have vitamins.") Can respond to peers' suggestions in group discussions on nutrition (e.g., "If you eat enough fruits and vegetables, you will stay healthy." "Yes, I agree, eating vegetables helps us stay healthy.").
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of various Panamanian dishes (e.g., sancocho, arroz con pollo, fruit salad, empanadas) and asks students: "Which of these dishes do you think are healthy? Why?" Students share their initial ideas in pairs for 1 minute, then volunteer answers to the class, activating prior knowledge about food choices and justifying opinions (e.g., "I think fruit salad is healthy because it has vitamins.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models giving advice and explaining benefits using the target grammar. For example, "You should eat more fruits because they provide vitamins for your body." The teacher clearly articulates "metabolism" and "antioxidant," stressing the correct syllables. The teacher also highlights the difference between /ɪ/ in "bit" (little food) and /iː/ in "beat" (to beat an egg) while explaining basic food preparation. The teacher then models how to offer advice respectfully: "If you want to improve your digestion, you should try adding more fiber to your diet."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and concepts through questions and examples.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'balanced diet' mean? Does it mean eating only one type of food?" (Expected answer: No, it means eating a variety of foods from all groups.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'You should eat more vegetables,' am I giving an order or advice?" (Expected answer: Advice.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nutrition")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents short dialogues (audio or written) between two teenagers discussing their meal choices and a simple recipe for a healthy snack (e.g., a fruit smoothie or yogurt parfait). The dialogues incorporate target vocabulary (e.g., 'nutrients,' 'hydration,' 'protein,' 'carbohydrates,' 'improve,' 'prefer,' 'recommend') and grammar (e.g., "You should avoid processed foods," "If you drink water, you will stay hydrated"). The recipe provides steps for preparation using simple verbs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing preferences and opinions about food, giving and asking for dietary advice, and understanding simple instructions related to food preparation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to/read the dialogues and recipe. Then, they answer gist questions orally in pairs, followed by a quick class check.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"According to the dialogue, what kind of food does Maria prefer?" (Gist)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the first step to make the fruit smoothie?" (Information matching/recall)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: The dialogue suggests avoiding all carbohydrates." (True/False)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking practice using modal "should" for giving advice, first conditional for facts (If... will), and adverbial clauses for justification (because it...). Integration of vocabulary: nutrition, health, benefits, balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, grains, vitamins, minerals, protein, carbohydrates, hydration, eat, prefer, improve, consume, advise, healthy, balanced, nutritious, beneficial.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                        1.  <b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Builder (5 min):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or a handout, and students complete them orally with a partner, focusing on the target structures and vocabulary.
                        <br>Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You ______ eat more fruits ____ they are full of ______." (should, because, vitamins/nutrients)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you _______ a balanced diet, you _______ energy to _________ all day." (eat, will have, improve)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Good ______ is important _______ it helps your ______ function correctly." (nutrition, because, metabolism)</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher monitors and corrects pronunciation, especially stress in words like "nutrition" and "metabolism."
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                        2.  <b>Semi-Controlled Practice - "Give Advice" Chain Drill (8 min):</b> Students sit in a circle or rows. The teacher starts with a common health problem (e.g., "I feel tired often."). The first student gives advice using "You should..." and "because it...". The next student offers alternative advice or agrees/disagrees using the first conditional.
                        <br>Example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "I feel tired often."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 1: "You should eat more grains because they provide carbohydrates for energy."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 2: "Yes, I agree. If you eat whole grains, you will have sustained energy."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 3: "You should also try to consume more water. If you drink enough, you will improve your hydration."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher guides and ensures all students get a chance to speak and use the target structures. Focus on minimal pairs for long/short vowels (e.g., "live/leave" if applicable to food choices, or "bit/beat" for precision).
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">
                        3.  <b>Pair Interaction - "Recipe Exchange" (7 min):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student imagines they have a simple, healthy recipe (e.g., a fruit salad, a vegetable stir-fry, a whole-wheat sandwich). They orally explain the steps to prepare their recipe to their partner, focusing on clarity and using action verbs. After explaining, they ask their partner for feedback or advice related to making it even healthier or tastier. Partners use "You should..." or "If you add..., it will..." to respond.
                        <br>Example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "To make my healthy fruit salad, first, chop fresh pineapples, mangos, and papayas. Then, mix them in a bowl."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Sounds delicious! You should add a squeeze of orange juice because it gives extra flavor and vitamins. If you use organic fruits, it will be even more beneficial."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nutrition")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion and Presentation - "Healthy School Lunch Menu Debate".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group's task is to collaboratively design a healthy and balanced school lunch menu for one week, considering typical Panamanian eating habits and promoting good nutrition. They must prepare to present their menu and justify their choices. They should include at least two recipes for simple healthy meals. The discussion and presentation must incorporate:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Giving dietary advice ("You should include more vegetables because they provide fiber and vitamins.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explaining nutrition benefits ("If we eat a mix of foods, it will give us energy and help us stay healthy.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explaining recipe steps ("To prepare the lentil soup, first chop the carrots and potatoes.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responding to peers' suggestions ("Yes, I agree, eating vegetables helps us stay healthy." or "I prefer to avoid processed snacks, because they are high in calories.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Discussing how their healthy menu might differ from traditional Panamanian celebration meals, addressing the sociolinguistic aspect (e.g., "While 'arroz con pollo' is delicious for celebrations, for daily school lunch, we should prioritize lighter options like salads or whole grains because they promote better digestion.").</li>
                    </ul>
                    After the group discussion, each group presents their menu to the class (2-3 minutes per group). The class can ask questions and offer additional advice.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, communicative task completion, and correct usage of target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussion and presentation, focusing on speaking fluency and accuracy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Healthy School Lunch Menu Debate" group discussion and present their menu to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Speaking):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Fluency and Clarity:</b> Speaks at an understandable pace; ideas are clear and easy to follow.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation; correct stress on multisyllabic words (e.g., "carbohydrate," "vitamin"); differentiation of minimal pairs (e.g., /ɪ/ vs /iː/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct use of "should" for advice, first conditional for facts, and adverbial clauses for justification.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Effectively uses target vocabulary related to nutrition and healthy food (e.g., 'balanced diet,' 'nutrients,' 'fiber,' 'protein,' 'organic,' 'beneficial').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interaction:</b> Actively responds to peers' suggestions and opinions in group discussions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new piece of advice about healthy eating you gave or received today using 'should'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining a recipe or giving advice was easiest/most challenging for you today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now explaining why certain foods are healthy and giving dietary advice in English?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you will do to improve your speaking fluency about nutrition outside of class?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking-focused lesson? (e.g., student engagement in role-play, clear application of grammar).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with expressing justification or using the first conditional spontaneously? (e.g., more sentence frames, specific prompt cards).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I ensure the pronunciation focus (word stress, minimal pairs) is consistently integrated and assessed?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The speaking practice in this lesson, particularly on explaining nutrition benefits and giving advice, will serve as a strong foundation for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition). Students will use the same vocabulary and grammatical structures to write short paragraphs or a blog post recommending healthy eating habits and explaining their benefits, perhaps even designing a written version of their healthy school menu with justifications.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>